// 17 — RULE: Microsoft's own published signatures for this campaign fired
//
// Tables       AlertInfo / AlertEvidence
// Confidence   HIGHEST in the set. This is not our inference about behavior; it is
//              Microsoft's own verdict on a sample, delivered by the vendor who published
//              the campaign analysis.
// Source       Microsoft Security Blog, 2026-08-04, "ChainDrop supply chain compromise:
//              anatomy of a self-propagating worm" — detections section.
// Last run     2026-08-07, 30-day lookback. See RESULT below.
//
// WHY A RULE FOR ALERTS WE WOULD ALREADY BE SENT
//
// Because being sent an alert and noticing it are different things. These signature names
// land in the same queue as everything else Defender raises. This query pulls exactly the
// campaign's own detections out of that queue and puts them on the hunt's own report, so a
// hit cannot be lost to alert volume during the window when it matters most.
//
// Behavior:Win32/SuspBunActivity.A is the load-bearing one for this estate, because this
// estate is 2,831 reporting Windows 11 devices and 158 Windows Servers.
//
// Read that signature's existence precisely (doctrine §0.6(a)). Provable: Microsoft
// published a Win32 variant alongside the Linux one, so the vendor considered the Windows
// execution path worth shipping a behavioral detection for. NOT provable from that fact:
// that the Windows path has been observed in the wild, or that it has been observed here.
// An earlier draft of this comment claimed the signature was "independent confirmation
// that the Windows bun.exe path is real" — it is confirmation of what Microsoft chose to
// detect, which is a weaker and different statement. The rule is worth running either way;
// the justification has to be the one the evidence supports.
//
// THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS RULE COULD NEVER HAVE FIRED
//
// It filtered on `AlertInfo.ThreatFamilyName`, which does not exist. `AlertInfo` carries
// nine columns and that is not one of them; the threat family lives on `AlertEvidence` and
// is spelled `ThreatFamily`. Graph rejects the query outright:
//
//     400 BadRequest — 'where' operator: Failed to resolve table or column or scalar or
//     graph expression named 'ThreatFamilyName'
//
// It was committed as coverage, lint-clean, and never executed. That is the exact failure
// doctrine §0.6(a) exists to stop: an unrun rule contributes nothing and reads on the page
// as though it contributes everything. A rule is not coverage until it has returned rows —
// including zero rows — from the tenant.
//
// CONTROL (doctrine §0.1) — run both; their answers gate this rule's zero. The rule has
// two independent branches and they need separate controls, because one of them turns out
// to be far weaker than the other on this tenant.
//
//     AlertInfo | where Timestamp > ago(30d)
//     | summarize AlertRows = count(), DistinctAlerts = dcount(AlertId)
//
//     AlertEvidence | where Timestamp > ago(30d) | where isnotempty(ThreatFamily)
//     | summarize Rows = count(), Families = dcount(ThreatFamily)
//
// RESULT — executed 2026-08-07, 30-day lookback. 0 rows.
//
// Control A (Title branch): AlertInfo carries 2,034 rows / 2,029 distinct alerts over the
// same window. The table is live and Title is populated, so the Title branch's zero is a
// measured absence: none of the five signature names, and none of the four family stems,
// appears in any alert title on this tenant in 30 days.
//
// Control B (ThreatFamily branch): FAILED, and this is the honest part. AlertEvidence has
// exactly 2 rows with a non-empty ThreatFamily across 30 days, carrying 1 distinct value.
// The column is essentially never written here. A zero from that branch is therefore not
// readable as "no campaign family was seen" — it is indistinguishable from "the column is
// empty", which is doctrine §0.1's definition of an unusable zero.
//
// So the defensible claim from this run is the narrow one: Microsoft's published signature
// names have not appeared in an alert title on this tenant. The ThreatFamily branch stays
// in the rule because it costs nothing and will become meaningful if AV detections start
// populating the column, but it contributes no coverage today and must not be counted as
// though it does.

let CampaignSignatures = dynamic([
    "Trojan:NPM/ShaiLoader.BY",
    "Trojan:NPM/MalBun.A",
    "Trojan:NPM/ShaiWorm.DAY!MTB",
    "Behavior:Linux/SuspBunActivity.A",
    "Behavior:Win32/SuspBunActivity.A"
]);
// Matched on the family stems as well as the full signature names, because Microsoft
// revises the suffix (.A, .B, !MTB) on the same family without announcing it, and an exact
// match on today's five strings goes stale the first time they ship a variant.
let CampaignFamilies = dynamic(["ShaiLoader", "MalBun", "ShaiWorm", "SuspBunActivity"]);
// Both tables are searched and the AlertIds unioned. The signature name surfaces in
// AlertInfo.Title when the detection came from EDR behavior, and in
// AlertEvidence.ThreatFamily when it came from AV — matching one table alone silently
// halves the rule.
let MatchedAlerts = union
    (AlertInfo
     | where Timestamp > ago(30d)
     | where Title has_any (CampaignSignatures) or Title has_any (CampaignFamilies)
     | project AlertId),
    (AlertEvidence
     | where Timestamp > ago(30d)
     | where ThreatFamily has_any (CampaignFamilies)
     | project AlertId)
    | distinct AlertId;
AlertInfo
| where Timestamp > ago(30d)
| where AlertId in (MatchedAlerts)
| join kind=leftouter (
    AlertEvidence
    | where Timestamp > ago(30d)
    | project AlertId, EvidenceRole, EntityType, DeviceName, FileName, SHA256, ThreatFamily,
              AccountName, AccountUpn, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP, ProcessCommandLine
) on AlertId
| project Timestamp, AlertId, Title, Severity, Category, ThreatFamily, ServiceSource,
          DetectionSource, AttackTechniques, DeviceName, EntityType, EvidenceRole, FileName,
          SHA256, AccountName, AccountUpn, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP, ProcessCommandLine
| order by Timestamp desc
| limit 1000
